<p>Closed choices: each entry says what it does, what it rules out, and why, from the settled decisions (Appendix A) and principles (§0). Reopening one needs an argument; the compiler's message follows where it enforces one.</p>

<h2 id="principles">Principles behind the choices</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>One spelling per construct:</strong> prose may paraphrase and elide; code may not.</li>
<li><strong>Safety is the default; bypasses are loud:</strong> each carries a <code>because</code> clause, and <code>bayan audit</code> lists them.</li>
<li><strong>Read order matches meaning.</strong> A declaration starts with what it is, then its name.</li>
<li><strong>The compiler does the tedious work:</strong> formatting, canonical spelling, diagnostics, not the author.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="syntax">Syntax</h2>

<h3 id="no-semicolons">No semicolons</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> A statement ends at its line; newline rules are fixed and small.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> <code>;</code> as a terminator or separator in source.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> An unneeded terminator is noise. The editor still accepts <code>;</code> as an input form; <a href="ref-cli.html#format"><code>bayan format</code></a> rewrites it.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1040:</span> `;` is an input form; a statement ends at the newline
  --&gt; main.byn:2:10

  2 │ let b = 2; print(a + b)
    │          ^ remove `;`
    │
  help: put the next statement on its own line
    │ 2 │ let b = 2
    │ 3 │ print(a + b)</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1040</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="one-comment-form">Only <code>//</code> comments</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> Comments start with <code>//</code> and run to the line end.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Block comments.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Block comments nest badly, hide code and blend into code when scanning.</p>

<h3 id="head-first">Head-first declarations</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> Kind first, modifier after: <code>struct unique</code>, <code>type distinct</code>, <code>union error</code>, <code>struct attribute</code>, <code>enum open</code>. Fields, parameters, returns, payloads: type first; a local: name first (<code>let total = 0</code>).</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> A modifier before the keyword, <code>name: Type</code>, and <code>-&gt;</code> outside function types.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A reader learns what a declaration is before its name; declarations of a kind line up.</p>

<h3 id="rune">The text unit is <code>rune</code></h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> A Unicode scalar value's type is <code>rune</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> <code>char</code>: the name suggests a byte or 16-bit unit, and a scalar is neither.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Text bugs come from confusing bytes, units, characters; the name should not blur them.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1048:</span> `char` is now `rune`
  --&gt; main.byn:1:14

  1 │ bool isVowel(char c) =&gt; c == 'a' or c == 'e'
    │              ^^^^ the input form
    │
  help: rename the type to `rune`
    │ 1 │ bool isVowel(rune c) =&gt; c == 'a' or c == 'e'
    │   │              ++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1048</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="no-string-plus">No <code>+</code> on strings</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> Strings join by interpolation (<code>"Hello, \(name)"</code>) or one literal.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> <code>a + b</code> with two strings.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Repeated <code>+</code> allocates each step and hides the cost; interpolation builds once and shows the shape.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2209:</span> strings cannot be joined with `+`; use interpolation
  --&gt; main.byn:2:12

  2 │ let path = "/usr" + "/lib"
    │            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write one literal: `"/usr/lib"`
    │ 2 │ let path = "/usr" + "/lib"
    │   │                 -----</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2209</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="postfix-if">Postfix <code>if</code> and <code>unless</code></h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> A one-statement condition can follow the statement; <code>and</code>/<code>or</code>/<code>not</code> and symbolic spellings both exist, and <code>style.booleans</code> picks one.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> A per-file or per-line choice between boolean spellings, and <code>stmt unless not cond</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Early exits read as sentences; one codebase reads one way.</p>

<h3 id="no-question-suffix">No <code>?</code> suffix on names</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> <code>?</code> means only <code>T?</code>, <code>?.</code>, <code>??</code>, the ternary.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Boolean members such as <code>isEmpty?</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> One symbol, one meaning: optionality.</p>

<h3 id="null-checks">Null is tested with <code>is null</code></h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> Test an optional with <code>is null</code> or <code>is not null</code>, or use <code>?.</code> and <code>??</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> <code>== null</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Equality is for values; null is their absence.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2027:</span> compare with `is null` / `is not null`, not `==`
  --&gt; main.byn:3:13

  3 │     let c = n == null
    │             ^^^^^^^^^
    │
  help: write `n is null`
    │ 3 │     let c = n is null
    │   │               ++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2027</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="no-c-for">No three-part <code>for</code>, no <code>goto</code></h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> Loops iterate a range or collection, or are <code>while</code> loops.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> <code>for (init; test; step)</code> and <code>goto</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Three-part loops breed off-by-one bugs; ranges show the bounds.</p>

<h3 id="no-lambda-sigil">No <code>@</code> lambda shorthand, no <code>mut</code></h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> The implicit argument is <code>it</code>; a method changing its receiver says <code>ref this</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> <code>@</code> as a lambda shorthand, and a <code>mut</code> keyword.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>@</code> belongs to attributes; <code>ref this</code> tells reader and compiler the receiver can change.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2115:</span> `bump` modifies `this` but is not declared `ref this`
  --&gt; main.byn:5:9

  5 │         n += 1
    │         ^ `this` is modified here
    │
  help: declare it `ref this` as the first parameter
    │ 4 │     void bump(ref this) {
    │   │               ++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2115</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2 id="values">Values and types</h2>

<h3 id="let-var">Bindings are immutable unless declared <code>var</code></h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> <code>let</code> binds once; <code>var</code> allows reassignment and warns when never reassigned.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Assigning to a <code>let</code>; silent mutability.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A reader can trust a <code>let</code> without scanning the function.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2104:</span> `total` is immutable
  --&gt; main.byn:2:1

  2 │ total += 1
    │ ^^^^^ declared with `let` on line 1
    │
  help: declare it with `var` if it should change
    │ 1 │ var total = 0
    │   │ +++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2104</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="units">Units are types, not angle brackets</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> A unit is a distinct type, converting implicitly only to a finer one; a bare literal does not fit a distinct type. Mixed-currency addition traps; <code>tryAdd</code> and a failing <code>add</code> recover.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> <code>5&lt;s&gt;</code>, <code>int&lt;Bytes&gt;</code>, a bare <code>0</code> for unit types, <code>per</code> for units, and automatic <code>T.Count</code> or <code>T.Index</code> types.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Angle brackets collide with generics; counts (<code>length</code>, <code>runeCount</code>) are plain <code>i64</code>, and units apply to slice offsets, where mixing costs most.</p>

<h3 id="requires">Validation is <code>requires</code> on the type</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> A type states its invariant with <code>requires</code>, checked at construction: one naming only <code>it</code> belongs to the field above; one naming other fields is struct-level.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> <code>T.Validated</code>, <code>validated(T)</code>, and checks scattered over every caller.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> If a value of the type exists, it is valid. A field of a struct with <code>requires</code>, <code>states</code> or <code>@sealed</code> is writable only from its <code>ref this</code> methods or through <code>with</code>.</p>

<h3 id="with">One copy-with-changes form</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> <code>x with { f: v }</code> is the only copy-with-changes form.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Derived <code>withF(v)</code> methods, and <code>with</code> as the effect-handler keyword.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> One spelling, no generated API.</p>

<h3 id="typestate">Typestate in a <code>states</code> block</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> A type lists its states; methods say which they need and which they <code>become</code>; <code>state</code> is a keyword only at a member declaration's start.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Runtime "is it open yet" flags for compiler-checkable protocols.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>write</code> on a closed file is a compile error.</p>

<h3 id="views-copies">Views and copies have fixed names</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> Fixed names: <code>span</code>, <code>mutableSpan()</code>, <code>copy()</code>, <code>T(x)</code>; size is <code>length</code>, <code>count(p)</code> counts predicate matches; <code>string(x)</code> always formats.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> <code>toX</code>/<code>asX</code> members, <code>fromUtf8</code>, <code>count</code> as a size.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The name tells a cheap view from an allocation. Bytes decode through codecs: <code>try Utf8.decode(bytes)</code> or <code>Utf8.tryDecode(bytes)</code>. Integers format with <code>string(n, as: Radix)</code>; hex and base64 are codecs.</p>

<h3 id="named-args">Arguments are named</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> Arguments carry their parameter name unless a particle stands in: <code>from to until through at by for as</code>, at most once per signature; <code>at</code> over <code>on</code>, and <code>for</code> only in label position.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Positional calls with several same-typed arguments.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> <code>copy(from: a, to: b)</code> cannot be called backwards.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2305:</span> argument for `verbose` must be named
  --&gt; main.byn:4:6

  4 │ show(true)
    │      ^^^^
    │
  help: write `verbose: …`; a `bool` argument says nothing at the call site
    │ 4 │ show(verbose: true)
    │   │      +++++++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2305</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="no-inheritance">No inheritance</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> Behaviour is shared through interfaces and monomorphized generics.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Class inheritance, until a GC tier exists to make it safe.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> It needs object lifetimes the language does not manage.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2802:</span> `Box.count` is `i32 count` but `ICount` requires `i64 count { get }`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:9

  6 │     i32 count
    │         ^^^^^ does not match `ICount`
    │
  help: change the field's type to `i64`</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN2802</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="json">JSON is library, not syntax</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> JSON is a first-class standard-library feature; decoding yields a trusted <code>T</code> only when every field is a <code>requires</code> type or non-text scalar.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> A JSON literal syntax in the language.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Data formats change faster than grammars.</p>

<h2 id="errors">Errors and effects</h2>

<h3 id="fails">Failure is part of the signature</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> A function that can fail says <code>fails E</code>; a caller writes <code>try</code> (covering the expression to its right) or <code>catch</code>. Error types are <code>union error</code>; a failing function exits with <code>fail .case</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Exceptions, the source <code>!</code> shorthand (<code>User!E</code> is an editor input form only), the older <code>error set</code> spelling, and returning an error case as an ordinary value.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Every failing path is visible at the call site and in the type.</p>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1056:</span> `return .missing` returns an error; write `fail .missing`
  --&gt; main.byn:6:5

  6 │     return .missing if key.isEmpty
    │     ^^^^^^ this leaves with an error
    │
  help: leave with the error: `fail .missing`
    │ 6 │     fail .missing if key.isEmpty
    │   │     ++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler's message for this program. <code>bayan explain BYN1056</code> prints the long form.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h3 id="unused-values">An unused value is an error</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> A non-void statement expression must be bound, returned or discarded with <code>let _ =</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Silently ignoring a result.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Ignored results are how failures get lost.</p>

<h3 id="except-patterns">Pattern rules</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> <code>except</code> takes patterns only; patterns can be named.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Arbitrary expressions in <code>except</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> One mechanism for taking values apart.</p>

<h3 id="uses">Effects are a closed list after <code>uses</code></h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> A function lists its needed capabilities after <code>uses</code>; <code>provide</code> installs handlers, and <code>Panic.handler</code> is an effect handler using <code>console</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> A symbol for effects, <code>noalloc</code> and <code>nosuspend</code> modifiers, <code>panic</code> as an effect, and open-ended effect names.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A closed list can be checked and printed.</p>

<h3 id="because">Every bypass says <code>because</code></h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> Anything overriding a safety rule carries a <code>because</code> clause, e.g. <code>x.trustUnchecked() because "…"</code>; secrets need <code>expose … because</code> in derivations, and <code>bayan audit</code> lists every one.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> A bare string as the purpose clause, and bypasses with no reason.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> A reviewer reads the reasons without opening each file. Two trust layers: outside values are untrusted until validated, and the bypass names its reason.</p>

<h3 id="fibers">Fibers and borrows</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> A struct holding a mutable reference is <code>unique</code>. No reference crosses into a fiber that may outlive it; keywords: <code>async</code>, <code>await</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Sharing a mutable borrow with a task that can outlive its owner.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> It removes a class of data races without a runtime check.</p>

<h2 id="names">Names and files</h2>

<h3 id="naming">Naming grammar</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> BYN50xx rules apply to all code; BYN51xx to a package's public API. Domain words go in <code>grammar</code>. The source extension is <code>.byn</code>; the language is Bayan. Operator members use an <code>Op</code> suffix.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Per-team naming arguments, and a separate linter configuration.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> The compiler enforces the names, so reviewers need not; see the <a href="ref-diagnostics.html">diagnostics index</a>.</p>

<h3 id="canonical-form">One canonical form on disk</h3>
<p><strong>Choice.</strong> <code>bayan format</code> rewrites input forms into the single canonical spelling.</p>
<p><strong>Excludes.</strong> Configurable style, apart from <code>style.booleans</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why.</strong> Diffs show changes in meaning, never taste.</p>

<h2 id="planned">Planned</h2>
<p>The specification lists designed features not in the compiler: not dropped, only unavailable. See <a href="ref-std.html#planned">the standard library's planned section</a> for the library side. The spec's <code>trust(value, "reason")</code> is spelled <code>trustUnchecked() because "…"</code> in the compiler.</p>
